  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1213 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nIn the foothills and areas west of I-25 (except Larimer County \nwhere clouds slowed warming), temperatures have warmed into the \nmid to upper 70s already, reaching their convective temperatures. \nThunderstorms have formed and are moving across parts of the \nDenver metro area. MLCAPE for the urban corridor is 300-700 J/kg, \nso the storms are expected to stay sub-severe. There is also some \ndrier air over eastern Boulder and Larimer Counties where dew \npoints have fallen into the 40s. We lowered PoPs slightly for the \nwest side of the urban corridor because of this. Hi-Res models \nstill favor numerous thunderstorms for the eastern plains this \nafternoon and evening, but they have been inconsistent regarding \nstorm evolution and timing. There's still a threat for severe \nthunderstorms over the eastern plains, south of I-76 with large \nhail and winds up to 60 mph. If northern parts of the urban \ncorridor miss this first batch of storms, a second batch could \nform later this afternoon. By mid evening (8-9PM), storms are \nexpected to be finished, expect a few possible stragglers over the\nfar eastern plains.\u00a0\n\nShowers will be possible tomorrow\u00a0morning across northern \nColorado due to lift ahead of an upper level low moving across \nWyoming. These showers could continue into the afternoon over the \nnortheast corner of the state. The airmass will continue\u00a0to cool \nwith the low nearby. Highs are expected to be in the mid 60s to \nlower 70s. The cooler temperatures are expected to be found over \nthe northeast plains where cloud cover is anticipated most or all \nof the day. Models have backed off on cloud cover some for \ntomorrow afternoon along the I-25 corridor. Where temperatures \nreach the lower 70s, MLCAPE is expected to reach 300-800 J/kg, and\nbe slightly higher along and east of the Palmer Divide. Storm \nchances tomorrow will be lower due to less moisture, subsidence, \nand possible capping. Though if storms can form, a couple severe \nones are possible over and east of the Palmer Divide where better \ninstability will reside.\u00a0\n\nThe upper level low weakens and shifts east of the region on \nMonday. Replacing it, will be shortwave ridging. Temperatures will\nbe slightly warmer than Sunday, but a little below normal with \nhighs in the lower to mid 70s across northeast Colorado. Looking \nupstream, a closed low will move across southern California on \nMonday. This low begins to weaken Tuesday as it runs into the \nridge. The low lifts northeastward Tuesday and Tuesday night. On \nWednesday, this now open trough will be over Wyoming. As it passes\nnorth of Colorado, it could bring scattered showers and \nthunderstorms sometime Tuesday afternoon through Tuesday night. If\nit slows slightly, we could see them on Wednesday.\u00a0\u00a0\n\nFor Wednesday through Saturday, the large scale weather pattern \nwill feature a large upper level ridge over the central part of \nthe country and a longwave trough off the west coast. For \nColorado, we'll see southwest flow for most of this\u00a0period with a \ncouple of minor fluctuations possible. Temperatures under this \npattern will be at or slightly above normal with highs in the mid \n70s to lower 80s. Chances for precipitation will mainly come from \nshortwave troughs and moisture lifting northward out of the \neastern Pacific and Mexico. There's very little model agreement \nidentifying these shortwave troughs, precipitation chances will be\nlow at this time.",
